# Contour Admin — Environment Variables
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vite bundles every VITE_ prefixed var into the production JS. They are
# readable by anyone who opens the admin panel in a browser. Treat them as
# public. The anon key is safe to ship — RLS + the user_roles table gate
# admin access. Operators still need to sign in.
#
# Real values go in .env (gitignored). This file is a template only.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Supabase ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# New-style projects use `sb_publishable_...`. Old projects still use the
# long-lived anon JWT. Either works — keep the variable name as ANON_KEY since
# that's what the Supabase JS client reads.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<your-project-ref>.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxx...   # or legacy anon JWT

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What you DON'T need here
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY — never. Service role bypasses RLS; bundling it
#   into the admin would mean any visitor's devtools can drain the database.
#   It lives in Supabase Vault (see migration 042_vault_secrets.sql) and is
#   auto-injected into edge functions by Supabase.
#
# Anything called "secret", "private", "server", "admin token", etc. — same
# rule. If you find yourself wanting one of these in the admin, the correct
# answer is almost always: write a SECURITY DEFINER RPC that does the action
# server-side and call that from the admin instead.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
